# ---------------------------------------------------------------- lessons
LESSONS = [
    {
        'id': 'security-concepts-and-controls',
        'title': 'Security Concepts, Controls and Everyday Practices',
        'summary': 'The core objectives, principles and control types that every other SSCP topic builds on, plus the operational habits (change control, baselines, data handling) that keep them working.',
        'diagram': None,
        'vocabIds': ['f1', 'f4', 'f6', 'f8', 'f12', 'f14', 'f16', 'f19'],
        'quizIds': ['q1', 'q2', 'q3', 'q5', 'q6', 'tf3'],
        'reading': (
            "Everything in information security comes back to three objectives. Confidentiality means information is seen only by those allowed to see it. Integrity means it is accurate and changes only through authorized actions. Availability means systems and data are usable when the business needs them. Almost every control you will ever configure serves at least one of these: encryption protects confidentiality, hashes and change control protect integrity, redundancy and backups protect availability. Two supporting ideas matter in practice. Accountability means every action can be traced to a single, uniquely identified person or process, and non-repudiation means that person cannot credibly deny it. A shared administrator login quietly destroys both, which is why auditors dislike it.\n\n"
            "A handful of design principles guide how controls are applied. Least privilege gives each user, process and system only the access needed for the job and only for as long as it is needed. Need-to-know applies the same thinking to information: even with a high clearance, you see only what your current duties require. Separation of duties splits a sensitive task, such as creating a vendor and paying that vendor, so no single person can complete a fraud alone. Because two people can collude, it is paired with job rotation and mandatory vacation, which let someone else notice irregularities. Defense in depth layers different kinds of controls so that one failure is not fatal, and zero trust goes further by refusing to treat network location as proof of trustworthiness.\n\n"
            "Controls can be described along two axes, and exam questions often use both. By function they are preventive (stop it), detective (spot it), corrective (fix it), deterrent (discourage it), compensating (substitute for a control you cannot use) and recovery (restore normal operation). By implementation they are administrative (policies, training, procedures), technical (firewalls, encryption, access control lists) and physical (locks, fences, guards, mantraps). A visible camera is a deterrent and a detective control but not a preventive one, because it cannot stop anybody opening a door.\n\n"
            "Written direction follows a hierarchy. A policy is the mandatory, high-level statement from management. A standard is a mandatory, specific requirement, such as full-disk encryption on all laptops. A procedure is the mandatory step-by-step method, and a guideline is recommended advice only. Alongside these sits the operational discipline that keeps systems predictable: change management ensures modifications are requested, assessed, approved, tested, implemented with a rollback plan and documented, and a configuration baseline gives you a known-good state to compare against so you notice drift.\n\n"
            "Finally, protect data according to what it is. The data owner classifies it, for example as public, internal, confidential or restricted, and the custodian implements the protections that follow. Retired media must be sanitized by clearing, purging or destroying it according to its sensitivity, as NIST SP 800-88 describes, because deleting a file only removes a pointer. Life safety also outranks asset protection, which is why electronic exit doors must fail safe rather than fail secure."
        ),
        'fundamentalsLabel': 'New to security concepts? See the everyday analogy',
        'fundamentals': "Think of a hospital. Confidentiality is the rule that only the treating team reads your chart. Integrity is making sure nobody quietly changes your medication record. Availability is the pharmacy being open at 3 a.m. Least privilege is why a cleaner has a key to the wards but not to the drug cabinet, and separation of duties is the rule that the nurse who prepares a controlled drug is not the one who signs it out. Defense in depth is the front door, the ward badge reader and the locked cabinet all working together, and the fire exit that unlocks when the alarm sounds is the fail-safe door: people come first, drugs second.",
        'keyTerms': ['CIA triad', 'Non-repudiation', 'Least privilege', 'Need-to-know', 'Separation of duties', 'Defense in depth', 'Compensating control', 'Configuration baseline', 'Data classification'],
        'commonTraps': [
            "Least privilege and need-to-know are not the same: need-to-know limits information, while least privilege limits all rights, including actions on systems.",
            "A symmetric MAC or a shared account cannot give non-repudiation, because more than one party holds the secret or the identity.",
            "Mandatory documents are policies, standards and procedures. Only guidelines are optional, so wording such as should or recommended signals a guideline.",
            "A control's category depends on its purpose. The same camera is a deterrent and detective control but never preventive, and a lock is preventive but not detective.",
            "The administrator who runs a server is a custodian, not the owner, so classification decisions are not theirs to change.",
        ],
        'scenario': "A finance team member can both add new suppliers and approve payments. A review finds a supplier that does not exist, paid every month for a year. The control that would have prevented this is separation of duties, and the control that would most likely have exposed it sooner is mandatory vacation or job rotation, because the fraud needed constant attention. The manager also finds that the application logs show only the shared finance admin account, so accountability for the changes cannot be established.",
        'onTheJob': "In practice, security fails more often from skipped basics than from clever attacks, so protect the boring habits. Keep every change flowing through a ticket with an approver other than the implementer, even when the change is urgent; emergency changes can be documented right after, but never skipped. Capture a configuration baseline the day a system goes live, not months later when nobody remembers what normal looked like. Challenge shared accounts every time you meet one, because they erase accountability and make investigations painful. When you are told to hide an incident or cut a corner that harms others, remember the ethics canons: put it in writing, escalate to the appropriate authority and do not take part. Finally, label data early. Teams that wait until an audit to classify information end up either protecting everything expensively or protecting nothing consistently.",
    },
    {
        'id': 'access-control-and-identity',
        'title': 'Access Control and Identity Management',
        'summary': 'How systems decide who you are and what you may do: authentication factors, SSO and federation, access control models and the identity lifecycle.',
        'diagram': 'sscpAuthProtocols',
        'vocabIds': ['f26', 'f27', 'f28', 'f31', 'f32', 'f35', 'f37', 'f40', 'f41'],
        'quizIds': ['q15', 'q16', 'q20', 'q24', 'q25', 'msq4'],
        'reading': (
            "Access control is a four-step conversation. Identification is the claim (I am Priya). Authentication is the proof. Authorization decides what the proven identity may do, and accounting records what it did. They must happen in that order, and a username on its own is identification, never authentication. Authentication proof comes from three families: something you know (password, PIN), something you have (hardware key, smart card, phone) and something you are (fingerprint, face). Multi-factor authentication needs factors from at least two different families, so a password plus a security question is two knowledge factors and does not count. Phishing-resistant methods such as FIDO2 security keys, passkeys and smart cards with PKI are the best choice for administrators, because they will not answer a look-alike site and cannot be relayed.\n\n"
            "Passwords still matter and are stored badly more often than stolen. Servers should never keep passwords or reversible encryptions of them. They should keep a salted hash produced by a deliberately slow function such as bcrypt, scrypt or Argon2, so that a stolen database costs attackers years of guessing rather than minutes. Current guidance favors long passphrases, checks against known-breached lists and avoids forced rotation without evidence of compromise. \n\n"
            "Single sign-on lets a user authenticate once and use many applications. Kerberos does it inside a domain using tickets and relies on synchronized clocks, while SAML and OpenID Connect do it across organizations through federation. A common exam trap: OAuth 2.0 is an authorization framework that lets an application act on your behalf with limited scope without handling your password, not an authentication protocol. SSO is convenient and reduces password fatigue, but it makes the identity provider a prize target, so protect it with strong MFA and monitoring. For network devices, RADIUS is typical for VPN and Wi-Fi user access, while TACACS+ suits administrative access because it separates authentication, authorization and accounting, encrypts the whole payload and supports per-command authorization.\n\n"
            "Authorization models answer who gets what. In discretionary access control the owner decides. In mandatory access control a central authority assigns labels and owners cannot override them. Role-based access control assigns rights to job roles and people to roles, which scales well. Attribute-based access control evaluates attributes of the user, resource and environment at request time, such as department, device health and time of day, and is the engine of many zero trust designs. \n\n"
            "Finally, access is a lifecycle, not an event. Accounts are provisioned from an authoritative source such as the HR system, changed when roles change, recertified by managers and data owners at regular intervals, and removed quickly when people leave. When someone changes jobs, old roles must be removed, or privilege creep follows. When an employee is dismissed, disable the account first and keep it for investigation instead of deleting it. Privileged and non-human accounts need extra care: vault their credentials, give them time-limited elevation, rotate secrets and never hard-code passwords in scripts."
        ),
        'fundamentalsLabel': 'New to access control? See the everyday analogy',
        'fundamentals': "Picture checking in at a hotel. Giving your name at the desk is identification. Showing your passport is authentication, and showing a passport and the credit card you booked with is stronger because the two proofs are different kinds. The key card the desk programs for room 412 and the gym is authorization, and the log of every door it opened is accounting. If you check out, the card is deactivated at once rather than left working for months. A conference badge that opens only the rooms booked for your event, and only during the event, is attribute-based access: who you are, where you want to go and when all matter.",
        'keyTerms': ['Authentication factors', 'Multi-factor authentication', 'FIDO2', 'Single sign-on', 'Federation', 'OAuth 2.0', 'RBAC', 'ABAC', 'Privilege creep'],
        'commonTraps': [
            "Two things you know are not multi-factor: a password plus a security answer is single-factor authentication used twice.",
            "OAuth 2.0 provides delegated authorization, not authentication. OpenID Connect is the layer that adds identity.",
            "Both role-based and rule-based access control abbreviate to RBAC. Role-based follows job function; rule-based applies a fixed rule list to everyone.",
            "On dismissal, disable the account and preserve it. Deleting it first destroys evidence and mailbox data.",
        ],
        'scenario': "An engineer moves from the finance application team to the marketing team. Six months later an access review shows she still holds finance roles and the marketing roles. The review also finds that three service accounts have passwords unchanged for years, written into scripts. The fixes are role-change deprovisioning and periodic recertification for the first problem, and vaulted, rotated secrets or managed identities with least privilege for the second.",
        'onTheJob': "Most access incidents come from lifecycle gaps, so automate the joiner, mover and leaver process from the HR source and review privileged groups monthly rather than annually. Do not accept a shared account for convenience: create named accounts and give administrators a separate privileged account that is never used for email or browsing. When rolling out MFA, enroll administrators and remote-access users first, and plan for lost-token recovery, because a weak help-desk reset process is how attackers bypass strong MFA. Watch for password spraying by counting failed logons across many accounts instead of per account, since lockout thresholds are exactly what sprayers stay beneath. Finally, test your identity provider's failure mode: if SSO goes down, make sure you still have a documented break-glass administrative path that is vaulted and monitored.",
    },
    {
        'id': 'risk-and-vulnerability-management',
        'title': 'Risk Management and Vulnerability Management',
        'summary': 'How practitioners identify, measure and treat risk, run vulnerability scans and penetration tests, and prioritize what to fix.',
        'diagram': 'sscpVulnCycle',
        'vocabIds': ['f49', 'f50', 'f51', 'f54', 'f56', 'f58', 'f59', 'f68', 'f69'],
        'quizIds': ['q29', 'q30', 'q31', 'q34', 'q36', 'tf13'],
        'reading': (
            "A threat is anything that could cause harm, a vulnerability is a weakness it could exploit, and risk is the likelihood of that happening multiplied by the impact on something of value. If any one of the three is absent, there is little or no risk, which is why an unpatched server on an isolated network can be lower risk than a patched one on the internet. Risk work begins by knowing what you have: an asset inventory, with owners and an idea of how important each asset is to the business. The business impact analysis then tells you which functions matter most, how long the business can survive without them and how much data it can afford to lose.\n\n"
            "Once risks are identified, each gets a treatment. You can mitigate by adding controls, transfer by buying insurance or shifting responsibility by contract, avoid by dropping the risky activity, or accept by knowingly living with it. Transfer shifts the financial cost but not accountability or reputation. Whatever remains after treatment is residual risk, and someone with authority must formally accept it. Everything is recorded in a risk register with a named owner for every entry. When numbers are available, quantitative analysis helps justify spending. Single loss expectancy is asset value multiplied by exposure factor, annualized rate of occurrence is how many times a year the event is expected, and annualized loss expectancy is the first multiplied by the second. If a control reduces the annualized loss expectancy by more than it costs each year, it is worth buying.\n\n"
            "Vulnerability management is the repeatable operational loop that finds weaknesses before attackers do. You scan, preferably with credentials so the scanner can read patch levels and settings from the inside, which gives more accurate results than a scan from outside. You validate findings because scanners produce false positives, then prioritize by real risk. The Common Vulnerability Scoring System gives a 0 to 10 severity rating and CVE gives each flaw a public name, but neither measures risk to your environment. Add asset value, exposure and whether the flaw is already being exploited, for example by checking the CISA Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalog. Then you patch or apply compensating controls, rescan to confirm and report.\n\n"
            "Penetration testing goes one step beyond scanning: an authorized tester tries to exploit weaknesses to show real impact. Black box testers start with nothing, gray box testers get something such as a standard user account, and white box testers get documentation and source code. Whatever the type, the first step is written authorization from someone entitled to give it, along with rules of engagement describing scope, targets, timing, allowed techniques, contacts and stop conditions. Testing without that paperwork can be a crime, however good the intentions."

        ),
        'fundamentalsLabel': 'New to risk management? See the everyday analogy',
        'fundamentals': "Think about whether to carry an umbrella. The threat is rain, the vulnerability is that your suit is not waterproof, and the risk is the chance of a downpour on your walk multiplied by how bad a ruined suit would be. You can mitigate by carrying a coat, transfer by dry-cleaning insurance, avoid by working from home, or accept getting a little wet. If a 200 dollar suit would be half ruined once every two years, a 10 dollar umbrella is plainly worth it, and that is annualized loss expectancy thinking. A weather forecast is your threat intelligence, and a doctor checking your lungs is the scan; the test of whether the cold actually makes you ill is the penetration test.",
        'keyTerms': ['Risk', 'Residual risk', 'Risk register', 'SLE, ARO and ALE', 'Credentialed scan', 'CVSS', 'CVE', 'Penetration test', 'Rules of engagement'],
        'commonTraps': [
            "CVSS measures technical severity, not risk to your organization. Two vulnerabilities with the same score can deserve very different priorities.",
            "Insurance transfers the financial impact only. It does not transfer accountability, legal duties or reputational damage.",
            "A vulnerability scan only finds potential weaknesses; a penetration test proves they can be exploited and needs prior written authorization.",
            "Single loss expectancy is not annualized. Multiply by the annualized rate of occurrence to reach ALE, and compare ALE reduction to the control's yearly cost.",
        ],
        'scenario': "A retailer's quarterly scan lists 400 findings. The security practitioner removes false positives by running credentialed scans, then ranks the rest using exposure and asset value, not CVSS alone. A critical flaw on the internet-facing payments portal that is being exploited elsewhere jumps to the top; an equally scored flaw on an isolated lab server is scheduled for the next maintenance window. After patching, a rescan confirms closure and the results go into a short report with time-to-remediate trends.",
        'onTheJob': "Do not let scanning become a ritual of printing reports no one reads. Agree remediation timeframes with system owners in advance, such as days for actively exploited internet-facing flaws and weeks for lower risk items, and track the numbers publicly. Keep an exceptions register for systems that cannot be patched, with a compensating control, an owner and an expiry date. Schedule scans so they do not knock over fragile legacy equipment, and always scan authenticated where you can. Before any penetration test, get the rules of engagement signed, make sure the operations team knows who to call if something breaks, and agree what happens if the testers find evidence of a real attacker. Document risk acceptances in writing; verbal acceptance disappears the day the person leaves.",
    },
    {
        'id': 'monitoring-logging-and-siem',
        'title': 'Monitoring, Logging and Detection',
        'summary': 'How to collect, protect and analyze logs, and how SIEM, intrusion detection, baselines and threat intelligence turn raw data into detections.',
        'diagram': 'sscpSiemPipeline',
        'vocabIds': ['f55', 'f60', 'f61', 'f62', 'f63', 'f64', 'f65', 'f66', 'f70'],
        'quizIds': ['q37', 'q38', 'q40', 'msq9', 'tf16', 'tf14'],
        'reading': (
            "You cannot investigate what you did not record, and you cannot trust a record that someone could edit. Good logging therefore has four parts. First, decide what to log: authentication successes and failures, privilege use, changes to configuration, access to sensitive data, security tool alerts, and network device and application events. Second, send logs off the host in near real time to a central, protected store, so an attacker who takes over a machine cannot erase the trail. Third, synchronize every clock with NTP and agree on a time zone, because correlating events across systems with drifting clocks produces timelines that mislead. Fourth, retain logs for as long as policy, regulation and investigations require, and protect them with integrity controls and restricted access.\n\n"
            "A security information and event management platform, or SIEM, collects logs from many sources, normalizes them into a common format, correlates events and raises alerts. Its power comes from correlation: one failed login is noise, but fifty failed logins across ten accounts followed by a success from a new country is a story. A SIEM is only as good as the sources feeding it and the tuning of its rules. Poor tuning creates alert fatigue, where analysts stop looking, and weak coverage leaves blind spots. Both need constant care.\n\n"
            "Detection technologies each have strengths. An intrusion detection system observes and alerts, usually fed by a span port or tap, while an intrusion prevention system sits inline and can block. Signature-based detection recognizes known bad patterns reliably but cannot see new attacks. Anomaly or behavior-based detection compares activity with a baseline of normal and can spot unknown attacks at the cost of more false positives. That is why a baseline taken while the environment is known to be clean is valuable, and why you re-baseline after legitimate major changes. On endpoints, endpoint detection and response tools record process, file and network activity and let analysts investigate and contain a host remotely.\n\n"
            "Every detection system makes two kinds of mistakes. A false positive is an alert on something harmless; it wastes time. A false negative is a missed attack; it creates false confidence, and it is usually the more dangerous error. Tuning moves the balance but never removes it, so layered detection, regular testing of detections and threat hunting matter. Threat intelligence feeds this work with indicators of compromise such as hashes, domains and addresses, but those go stale quickly. Descriptions of attacker behavior, such as those in the MITRE ATT&CK knowledge base, last much longer and make better detection ideas.\n\n"
            "Finally, monitoring supports management decisions. Continuous monitoring replaces annual snapshots with near real-time visibility of assets, vulnerabilities, configuration and events. Audits compare practice with a standard, ideally by an independent party. Reports to leadership work best when they show outcomes, such as mean time to detect and mean time to contain, rather than counts of alerts or gigabytes of logs collected."
        ),
        'fundamentalsLabel': 'New to security monitoring? See the everyday analogy',
        'fundamentals': "Imagine a shopping centre. Security cameras and door counters generate raw data, which is the logs. The control room that watches every screen together, notices that the same person has tried three different back doors, and phones the guards is the SIEM. A guard standing at a door who can tackle intruders is an intrusion prevention system, and a guard in the control room who only radios what they see is detection. Knowing what a normal Tuesday looks like is the baseline, so an unusual crowd at 3 a.m. stands out, and a wanted poster showing last month's thief's face is an indicator of compromise.",
        'keyTerms': ['SIEM', 'Log integrity', 'NTP', 'IDS and IPS', 'Signature-based detection', 'Anomaly detection', 'Baseline', 'False negative', 'Indicators of compromise'],
        'commonTraps': [
            "An IDS only alerts, and an IPS blocks. Only a device placed inline can block traffic; a span port copy cannot.",
            "A false negative, a missed real attack, is usually more dangerous than a false positive, so do not tune alerts down until real events disappear.",
            "Unsynchronized clocks break correlation. If an event timeline looks wrong, check time first.",
            "A SIEM does not create visibility by itself. Without relevant log sources and tuned rules it produces little useful detection.",
        ],
        'scenario': "An analyst notices that two servers' logon events are several minutes out of order, which makes a suspected intrusion timeline impossible to build. The cause is an unsynchronized clock. After fixing NTP, the SIEM correlation shows many failed logons across different accounts from one address, then a success, which points to password spraying. The analyst enriches the alert with threat intelligence, isolates the affected account and tunes the rule so the same pattern raises a higher-priority alert in future.",
        'onTheJob': "Start small and concrete: pick ten use cases, such as impossible travel, new administrator accounts and disabled logging, and make sure each one is collected, parsed and alerting before expanding. Test detections regularly with simulated attacks, because a rule that has never fired may be silently broken. Protect the logging pipeline itself; alert when a host stops sending logs, since attackers often switch off logging first. Keep a written runbook for each alert type so a tired analyst at 3 a.m. knows what to check and when to escalate. Review retention against legal advice, because keeping everything forever creates cost and discovery risk, while deleting too soon destroys evidence. And report outcomes, not activity, so leadership can see whether detection is actually improving.",
    },
]

LESSONS += [
    {
        'id': 'incident-response-forensics-recovery',
        'title': 'Incident Response, Forensics and Recovery',
        'summary': 'The incident lifecycle, how to collect evidence without destroying it, and how business continuity, disaster recovery and backups restore operations.',
        'diagram': 'sscpEvidenceCustody',
        'vocabIds': ['f71', 'f74', 'f75', 'f78', 'f79', 'f80', 'f83', 'f84', 'f85'],
        'quizIds': ['q41', 'q42', 'q44', 'q46', 'q48', 'q47'],
        'reading': (
            "An event is anything observable on a system or network; an incident is an event, or group of events, that actually or potentially harms security or breaks policy. Most alerts are events and never become incidents, so the first skill is triage: judge scope, affected assets, data sensitivity and business impact, then assign a severity that drives urgency and escalation. The accepted lifecycle has six stages. Preparation builds the plan, tools, contacts and trained team before anything happens. Detection and analysis confirm what is occurring. Containment stops the spread. Eradication removes the cause: malware, persistence mechanisms, rogue accounts and the vulnerability that let the attacker in. Recovery returns systems to service from known-good sources and watches for recurrence. Post-incident activity, the lessons-learned review, feeds improvements back into preparation. \n\n"
            "Containment decisions are where beginners hurt themselves. The instinct to pull the plug destroys memory contents, running processes and network connections, which are exactly the evidence you need. Isolate the host from the network instead, by switch port, host firewall or endpoint isolation, and keep it powered. During recovery, restoring from a backup taken after the compromise just reinstalls the attacker, so confirm the backup predates the intrusion and that the entry point is closed first.\n\n"
            "If evidence may be needed for HR, regulators or court, forensic discipline matters. Collect in order of volatility: registers and cache, memory, network state and processes, disk, then remote logs and archives. Take a bit-for-bit image of storage through a write blocker, record a cryptographic hash of the original and the copy to show they match, and do all analysis on the copy. Maintain the chain of custody: a written record of who held the evidence, when, where and why, with every transfer signed. A gap does not prove tampering but it gives the other side something to argue. When litigation is reasonably expected, legal counsel issues a legal hold that suspends routine deletion, including log rotation and backup overwrites.\n\n"
            "Recovery planning starts with the business impact analysis. Maximum tolerable downtime is how long a function can be out before harm becomes unacceptable. The recovery time objective is the target for restoring service and must be shorter than that limit. The recovery point objective is the most data, measured in time, that you can afford to lose and decides how often you back up or replicate. A hot site is ready within minutes or hours at the highest cost, a warm site has hardware but needs data and configuration restored, and a cold site offers space and power only. Choose the cheapest option that still meets the stated objectives.\n\n"
            "Backups come in three common types. Full backups copy everything. Incremental backups copy changes since the last backup of any kind, so they are quick to make, but a restore needs the full backup plus every incremental in order. Differential backups copy changes since the last full backup, so a restore needs just the full backup and the latest differential. Keep at least one copy offline or immutable, because ransomware hunts for backup repositories, and test restores regularly. "
        ),
        'fundamentalsLabel': 'New to incident response? See the everyday analogy',
        'fundamentals': "Think of how a good crew handles a kitchen fire. Training and extinguishers in the right places are preparation. Noticing smoke and working out whether it is the toaster or the fryer is detection and analysis. Shutting the door and cutting the gas stops the spread, which is containment, and you do not throw out the burned pan until the investigator has photographed it. Eradication is removing whatever caused the fire, recovery is rebuilding the kitchen and reopening, and the review afterwards asks why the alarm was slow. The restaurant's insurance paperwork, showing who touched what and when, is the chain of custody.",
        'keyTerms': ['Incident lifecycle', 'Containment', 'Eradication', 'Chain of custody', 'Order of volatility', 'Write blocker', 'MTD, RTO and RPO', 'Hot, warm and cold sites', 'Incremental and differential backups'],
        'commonTraps': [
            "Do not power off a compromised machine first. Isolate it from the network and capture volatile data before anything else.",
            "The recovery time objective must fit inside the maximum tolerable downtime, and the recovery point objective, not the RTO, decides backup frequency.",
            "Restoring an incremental chain needs the full backup plus every incremental; a differential restore needs only the full plus the latest differential.",
            "Choose the cheapest recovery site that meets the stated objectives. A hot site is not automatically the right answer.",
            "Post-incident reviews are blameless and aimed at improving controls, not at punishing the person who made the error.",
        ],
        'scenario': "At 7:40 on a Monday an analyst sees a workstation renaming files and showing a ransom note. She isolates it from the network, keeps it powered, captures memory and escalates to the incident commander. Backup administrators disconnect the backup repository from the domain while the team confirms the entry point was a phishing email. Systems are rebuilt from clean images and data restored from a copy that predates the infection, with the records database restored first because the business impact analysis ranks it highest. A week later the team meets to update detection rules and the phishing training.",
        'onTheJob': "Print the plan and keep an offline copy, because the day you need it your file server may be the thing that is encrypted. Know your three phone numbers before the incident: legal counsel, insurer and law enforcement liaison. Put a decision tree in the plan for the things people argue about under stress, such as when to isolate a production system and who can authorize it. Practise with tabletop exercises that include executives, because the hardest decisions, like whether to pay a ransom, belong to them and should not be debated for the first time during an attack. Treat backups as a product with an owner: test restores quarterly, time them against the recovery time objective and keep one copy that production credentials cannot touch. Record every action with a timestamp as you go; memory is unreliable at 3 a.m.",
    },
    {
        'id': 'cryptography-in-practice',
        'title': 'Cryptography in Practice',
        'summary': 'Symmetric and asymmetric encryption, hashing, signatures, certificates, TLS and the key management habits that decide whether any of it actually protects you.',
        'diagram': 'sscpCryptoUse',
        'vocabIds': ['f91', 'f92', 'f93', 'f95', 'f96', 'f97', 'f98', 'f100', 'f101'],
        'quizIds': ['q54', 'q55', 'q56', 'q57', 'q59', 'tf26'],
        'reading': (
            "Cryptography gives you four useful properties: confidentiality (only intended parties can read), integrity (changes are detected), authentication (you know who produced it) and non-repudiation (they cannot deny it). Different tools provide different properties, and most exam questions are about matching the tool to the property. Symmetric encryption, such as the Advanced Encryption Standard, uses one shared key to encrypt and decrypt. It is fast and suits large amounts of data, but the two parties must somehow share the key safely. Asymmetric encryption, such as RSA and elliptic curve cryptography, uses a mathematically linked pair: a public key anyone may have and a private key kept secret. It solves key distribution and enables signatures, but it is much slower. Real systems combine them: asymmetric methods agree on or protect a session key, and symmetric encryption protects the data.\n\n"
            "Remember the key directions, because they are easy to reverse under pressure. To send a confidential message, encrypt with the recipient's public key, so only their private key can open it. To sign, hash the message and encrypt the hash with your own private key, so anyone can verify with your public key. A digital signature therefore gives integrity, origin authentication and non-repudiation. A message authentication code such as HMAC mixes a shared secret into a hash. It proves integrity and that the sender knew the secret, but because both sides hold the secret it cannot give non-repudiation. A plain hash such as SHA-256 provides integrity only against accidental change, since anyone can recompute it after tampering. Hashing is one-way and keyless, so it is never reversed, and MD5 and SHA-1 are broken for collision resistance.\n\n"
            "Public key infrastructure answers the question of whose public key you really have. A certificate authority signs certificates that bind a public key to an identity, a registration authority vets requests, and clients trust the chain up to a root they already hold. Roots are kept offline in an HSM because losing one breaks everything beneath it. Certificates expire and can be revoked, either by a published certificate revocation list or by asking an online responder through OCSP. If a CA's key may be exposed, revoke it, rebuild with new keys and tell the people who rely on it. TLS uses all of this to protect data in transit. TLS 1.3 removes legacy ciphers and static RSA key exchange and requires ephemeral key exchange, giving forward secrecy, which means recorded traffic stays safe even if the server's long-term key is stolen later. Disable SSL and TLS 1.0 and 1.1.\n\n"
            "Finally, strong algorithms are defeated by weak key handling. Generate keys with a good random source, store them in a hardware security module or managed key vault rather than beside the data they protect, limit who can use them, rotate them on a schedule and immediately after suspected compromise, back them up carefully, and destroy them at the end of their life. Keep separate protections for data at rest, in transit and in use, because encrypting a disk does nothing for traffic crossing the network."
        ),
        'fundamentalsLabel': 'New to cryptography? See the everyday analogy',
        'fundamentals': "Think of a padlock factory. Symmetric encryption is a single key that locks and unlocks a box: fast, but you must somehow get a copy of the key to your friend. Asymmetric encryption is like handing out open padlocks: anyone can snap one onto a box for you (encrypt with your public key), but only you hold the key that opens it. A signature is like a wax seal pressed with a stamp only you own, which everyone can recognize but nobody can forge. A hash is a fingerprint of the contents, and a certificate authority is the notary who confirms that this stamp really belongs to you.",
        'keyTerms': ['Symmetric encryption', 'Asymmetric encryption', 'AES-GCM', 'Hash', 'HMAC', 'Digital signature', 'PKI and CA', 'Forward secrecy', 'Key management'],
        'commonTraps': [
            "Encrypt to a person with their public key; sign with your own private key. Reversing them is the most common mistake.",
            "HMAC cannot provide non-repudiation because both parties know the key. Only an asymmetric signature can.",
            "A hash alone does not protect against an attacker who can change the message and recompute the hash; you need a key (HMAC or signature).",
            "ECB leaks patterns and should not be used. Authenticated modes like GCM are preferred, and nonces must never repeat with the same key.",
        ],
        'scenario': "Two offices must send nightly 500 GB backups and have no shared secret. The engineer uses an asymmetric exchange to agree on a session key, encrypts the data with AES in GCM mode, and signs each backup manifest so the receiver can verify origin and integrity. The root CA key for the company's internal certificates sits offline in an HSM, and the issuing CA's certificates are revocable through OCSP. When a developer leaves, the engineer rotates the keys that person could access.",
        'onTheJob': "Do not invent cryptography. Use well-reviewed libraries and current protocol versions, and treat any custom scheme as a defect. Keep an inventory of certificates and their expiry dates, because outages from expired certificates are far more common than attacks on algorithms. Automate renewals where you can, and alert at 30 days. Inspect configurations with a scanner to find old protocol versions and weak cipher suites, and retire them deliberately with monitoring so you know which clients break. Never store keys in source code or configuration files; use a vault and short-lived credentials. Plan for key loss as carefully as key theft: with no backup or escrow of a data-encryption key, encrypted data is simply gone. And watch the horizon on quantum computing, since long-lived secrets encrypted today may need post-quantum algorithms later.",
    },
    {
        'id': 'network-security-controls',
        'title': 'Network and Communications Security',
        'summary': 'How networks work, how they are attacked, and the segmentation, firewall, VPN, wireless and monitoring controls that defend them.',
        'diagram': 'sscpNetPlacement',
        'vocabIds': ['f105', 'f106', 'f109', 'f110', 'f112', 'f113', 'f115', 'f116', 'f118'],
        'quizIds': ['q63', 'q64', 'q65', 'q66', 'q68', 'q69'],
        'reading': (
            "Start with the model. The seven-layer OSI model separates a network into physical signalling, data link (switches, MAC addresses, VLANs), network (routers, IP addresses), transport (TCP and UDP ports), session, presentation and application. The TCP/IP model groups these into four layers. Knowing where something sits tells you how to defend it: a switch-level attack such as ARP spoofing needs switch controls, a routing question belongs at layer 3, and a port or connection-state issue lives at layer 4. TCP is connection-oriented and reliable, built on a three-way handshake that SYN floods exploit by leaving connections half open, while UDP is connectionless, which makes spoofing and amplification attacks easier. \n\n"
            "Firewalls are the main traffic cops. A packet filter checks headers against rules, a stateful firewall tracks connections so replies are allowed automatically, and an application-layer or next-generation firewall inspects content and identifies applications and users. Rules are evaluated from the top and the first match wins, so specific rules go before general ones and the list ends in an explicit deny all. Firewalls work best with segmentation. A demilitarized zone holds internet-facing servers so that a compromised web server does not sit next to the database. VLANs separate broadcast domains but are not themselves a firewall: traffic between them must still be filtered. Microsegmentation applies the same idea to individual workloads, which matters when most traffic is server to server and never crosses a perimeter. Segmentation is the practical answer to lateral movement and ransomware spread.\n\n"
            "Admission and identity come next. IEEE 802.1X is port-based network access control: a device authenticates through the switch or access point to a RADIUS server before the port passes traffic, which stops an unknown laptop gaining access by plugging into a wall socket. Wireless security has moved from WEP, which is broken, through WPA2 with AES to WPA3, which resists offline password guessing. Enterprise modes authenticate each person with 802.1X so access can be revoked individually. \n\n"
            "Remote connections use VPNs. A site-to-site tunnel joins two networks between gateways, usually IPsec in tunnel mode with ESP, which provides encryption and integrity; the Authentication Header authenticates but does not encrypt. A remote-access VPN joins one client, and split tunnelling sends only corporate traffic through it, trading load for exposure to a hostile local network. A modern alternative is Zero Trust Network Access, which grants per-application access after checking user and device instead of putting the user on the whole network. Administrators should reach devices through SSH, from a hardened jump host on a management network, with AAA through TACACS+ or RADIUS.\n\n"
            "Attacks to recognize include denial of service, where distributed attacks use many sources and reflection abuses third parties, defended by rate limiting, SYN cookies and upstream scrubbing; man-in-the-middle and ARP spoofing, defended by dynamic ARP inspection, DHCP snooping and end-to-end encryption; and DNS abuse, including cache poisoning and tunnelling, where DNSSEC signs records but does not encrypt them. "
        ),
        'fundamentalsLabel': 'New to network security? See the everyday analogy',
        'fundamentals': "Picture an office building. The street door with a guard is the perimeter firewall, and the visitor lobby is the DMZ where the public may wait but cannot wander upstairs. Internal doors that need a badge are VLANs and internal firewall rules: just because you are inside does not mean you can open the server room. The badge reader that checks who you are before the lift moves is 802.1X. An armoured van between two company buildings is a site-to-site VPN, and an executive carrying a locked briefcase through the airport is a remote-access VPN. A fake reception desk across the road that looks exactly like yours is an evil twin.",
        'keyTerms': ['OSI model', 'Stateful firewall', 'DMZ', 'Segmentation', 'VLAN', '802.1X', 'IPsec ESP', 'WPA3', 'ZTNA'],
        'commonTraps': [
            "VLANs separate traffic but are not firewalls. Inter-VLAN routing still needs filtering.",
            "NAT hides addresses but is not a security control; a stateful firewall provides the protection people credit to NAT.",
            "AH authenticates but does not encrypt. ESP provides confidentiality and integrity, and tunnel mode is the usual choice between gateways.",
            "A span port copy cannot block traffic; only an inline device can prevent, and its fail mode matters.",
            "DNSSEC proves records are authentic but does not encrypt queries. Use DNS over TLS or HTTPS for privacy.",
        ],
        'scenario': "A company hosts a public web server, an email relay and an internal database. The engineer places the web and mail servers in a DMZ with firewall rules allowing only the specific ports and destinations required, and keeps the database in an internal VLAN that only the application tier can reach. Switch ports require 802.1X, wireless uses WPA3 Enterprise, and remote staff use ZTNA for the two internal web applications they need. Logs and flow records from the firewalls and switches go to the SIEM.",
        'onTheJob': "Review firewall rules every quarter and delete anything with no owner, no expiry or no recent hits; rule bases grow until nobody understands them. Always keep a console or out-of-band path to your network devices before you change access rules remotely, so you cannot lock yourself out. Back up device configurations and protect those backups, since they contain secrets. Test the failure modes of inline security devices before production: decide whether an overloaded IPS should fail open or closed and document who accepted that risk. When you introduce 802.1X, start in monitor mode and expect printers and cameras that cannot speak it, giving them a separate segment with tight rules. And remember that encryption cuts both ways: inspecting TLS lets you see malware but also gives your inspection device access to sensitive data, so scope and protect it carefully.",
    },
    {
        'id': 'systems-endpoint-cloud-application-security',
        'title': 'Systems, Endpoint, Cloud and Application Security',
        'summary': 'Malware and attacker behavior, hardening and patching, endpoint and mobile controls, virtualization and cloud security, and secure application practices.',
        'diagram': None,
        'vocabIds': ['f129', 'f130', 'f133', 'f134', 'f135', 'f138', 'f140', 'f144', 'f146'],
        'quizIds': ['q76', 'q77', 'q79', 'q81', 'q82', 'q85'],
        'reading': (
            "Malicious code is classified by how it spreads and what it does. A virus attaches to a host file and needs a user action to spread, a worm spreads by itself across networks, a trojan pretends to be legitimate software, ransomware locks or encrypts data for payment, a rootkit hides itself deep in the system and spyware gathers information quietly. Modern attackers increasingly avoid files completely. Fileless and living-off-the-land attacks run in memory and abuse legitimate tools such as PowerShell, so signature antivirus sees nothing, and defenders rely on behavior monitoring, script logging and endpoint detection and response. The most common way in remains social engineering: phishing, spear phishing, whaling, vishing and pretexting, countered by email filtering, MFA, authentication of mail domains with SPF, DKIM and DMARC, and a reporting channel staff actually use.\n\n"
            "Hardening reduces the attack surface. Start from a baseline such as a CIS Benchmark, remove software and services you do not need, close ports, disable default accounts, enable host firewalls and logging, and enforce disk encryption and secure boot. Patch management is the repeatable process of identifying, testing, deploying and verifying updates, with exceptions tracked and compensating controls for systems that cannot be patched. Application allowlisting, which permits only approved executables, is stronger than blocklisting for stable systems because it blocks the unknown by default. Endpoint detection and response adds visibility and remote containment, such as isolating a host, which ordinary antivirus lacks. For mobile devices, use device management with encryption, a work container and selective wipe, and keep Internet of Things devices on their own network segment.\n\n"
            "\n\n"
            "Virtualization and cloud shift responsibility rather than removing it. A Type 1 hypervisor runs on hardware, a Type 2 hypervisor runs on a host operating system, and a compromise of the hypervisor affects every guest, so patch it and keep its management interface on an isolated network. Watch for VM sprawl, which is unmanaged and unpatched machines accumulating, and for VM escape, which is rare but severe. Containers share the host kernel, so run them as non-root from minimal, scanned images and never bake secrets into images. In the cloud, the shared responsibility model says the provider secures the cloud itself and the customer secures what it puts in the cloud. In infrastructure as a service the customer patches the guest operating system. In software as a service the provider manages almost the whole stack. In every model the customer owns its data, identities and configuration, and most cloud breaches come from misconfiguration such as publicly readable storage. \n\n"
            "Applications are the last layer. Build security into the whole development lifecycle: requirements, threat modelling in design, secure coding, automated static and dynamic testing, and controlled release. The OWASP Top Ten is a useful checklist of common web flaws. SQL injection is prevented with parameterized queries, cross-site scripting with output encoding and a content security policy, and cross-site request forgery with anti-forgery tokens. "
        ),
        'fundamentalsLabel': 'New to systems security? See the everyday analogy',
        'fundamentals': "Imagine running a block of rented flats. Hardening is fitting every flat with the same decent locks, taking out the unused back doors and fixing the broken window latches. Patching is doing the maintenance round when the manufacturer announces a faulty lock. An allowlist is a doorman with a guest list rather than a list of known burglars. Moving to the cloud is like renting space in someone else's building: the landlord keeps the lifts and the roof safe, but you must still lock your own flat and not leave the door propped open, which is where most break-ins actually happen.",
        'keyTerms': ['Worm', 'Fileless malware', 'EDR', 'Application allowlisting', 'System hardening', 'MDM', 'Shared responsibility', 'Hypervisor', 'Parameterized queries'],
        'commonTraps': [
            "A worm spreads on its own and a virus needs a host file and a user action. The exam keys on the propagation method.",
            "In IaaS the customer, not the provider, patches the guest operating system. The provider handles the physical hosts and hypervisor.",
            "Parameterized queries stop SQL injection, while output encoding stops cross-site scripting. They are not interchangeable.",
            "Allowlisting blocks everything not approved; blocklisting only stops what is already known to be bad.",
            "Cloud exposure is usually customer misconfiguration, not provider failure, so the customer is accountable for its own storage policies.",
        ],
        'scenario': "A company moves its warehouse application to rented virtual machines and finds the images unpatched for months, a storage bucket readable by anyone with the link, and warehouse staff reading company email on personal phones. The practitioner removes public access from the bucket, builds a hardened image with a patch schedule and logging, enrolls phones in device management with a work container, and enables behavior-based endpoint detection. Application teams get static and dynamic testing in their build pipeline.",
        'onTheJob': "Fix exposure before elegance: public storage, unpatched internet-facing systems and missing multi-factor authentication give attackers their easiest wins, so tackle those first. Build hardened images once and deploy them everywhere rather than hardening machines by hand, and keep the image pipeline patched so new systems are never born vulnerable. Use cloud configuration scanning continuously, not annually, and make logging a default in every new account or subscription. Insist on an owner for every virtual machine and a decommission date for temporary ones, since sprawl is where forgotten, unpatched servers live. For mobile and IoT, accept that you cannot secure everything on the device, so segment and monitor instead. Finally, teach developers with real examples from your own code reviews; a thirty-minute walkthrough of an injection flaw they wrote lands better than any slide deck.",
    },
]
